<p>The variable <strong>joy1pressed</strong> (all lowercase) is filled in by a call
to <strong>readjoy1()</strong>. It holds the same five flags as joy1, for the
joystick in port 1, but a bit is set only on the reading where that direction
or button was <strong>first pressed</strong>. It goes back to 0 on the next reading and
does not set again until the direction has been released and pressed anew.</p>
<p>That makes it the right variable for one-shot actions - firing a single shot,
stepping through a menu, dropping one block - while joy1 suits movement that
should repeat while the stick is held.</p>
<p>Test it with the JOY_UP, JOY_LEFT, JOY_DOWN, JOY_RIGHT and JOY_FIRE masks.
Their bit values are not the same on every machine, so always use the constants
rather than raw numbers.</p>

<p>Example</p>
<code>
readjoy1();

// moves every frame the stick is held
if (joy1 &amp; JOY_LEFT) then x := x - 1;

// fires once per press, however long the button is held
if (joy1pressed &amp; JOY_FIRE) then Shoot();
</code>
<p>See also joy1 and readjoy1.</p>
